1. What are Keywords in JavaScript?
Keywords are special words that have specific meanings in JavaScript.They are part of the language syntax and cannot be used as variable names, function names, or identifiers.
Examples:let, var, const, if, else, for, while, return, break, continue, switch, case, default, try, catch, finally, class, function, typeof, delete, new, import, export.

2. What are Reserved Keywords in javascript?
Reserved keywords are words set aside by JavaScript for future use or special purposes.Even if they don’t currently perform a task, you still can’t use them as variable names.
Examples:enum, implements, package, protected, interface, private, public, static, yield, await, super.

3. Using let, var, and const

| Keyword | Scope           | Re-declaration | Re-assignment | Typical Use               |
| ------- | --------------- | -------------- | ------------- | ------------------------- |
| `var`   | Function-scoped | ✅ Yes          | ✅ Yes         | Older code or global vars |
| `let`   | Block-scoped    | ❌ No           | ✅ Yes         | Variables that may change |
| `const` | Block-scoped    | ❌ No           | ❌ No          | Constants or fixed values |

// var example
var name = "Naheem";
var name = "Mujaheed"; // allowed
console.log(name); // Mujaheed

// let example
let age = 25;
// let age = 30; ❌ not allowed
age = 30; // ✅ reassignment allowed
console.log(age);

// const example
const country = "Nigeria";
// country = "Ghana"; ❌ cannot reassign
console.log(country);

